# stale-verdict-watch.baseline — the PINNED known-red set.
#
# scripts/stale-verdict-watch.sh reds every 30 minutes for as long as a
# CONFLICTING pull request keeps asserting a green required verdict main has
# moved past. From 2026-08-08 to 2026-09-01 it did exactly that on run after
# run, mostly about ONE pull request (#11766, four required contexts green at
# 2026-08-17T10:0*Z, re-dispatching nothing after). A check that fails on every
# run for three weeks cannot warn: the NEXT stale verdict changes the log by a
# line and the conclusion by nothing.
#
# So this file pins the standing population, and the watch fails on the DELTA:
#
#   NOVEL   a reported pull request no line here covers   -> rc 1, the run FAILS
#   KNOWN   a reported pull request a line here covers    -> printed, counted,
#                                                            trended, NOT failed
#   HEALED  a line here whose pull request stopped being
#           reported (closed, rebased, or refreshed)      -> rc 8, the run FAILS
#           until the line is DELETED
#
# THAT LAST ARM IS THE POINT. This file may only shrink on its own. Growing it
# costs a commit, a review, and a written reason the parser refuses to accept
# as blank — and the moment a pinned row heals, the watch reds until somebody
# removes the line. An allowlist that grows quietly stops discriminating; this
# one cannot grow quietly and cannot be left down.
#
# FORMAT — one entry per line, all four fields mandatory:
#
#   <pr-number> <head-oid-prefix, 7-40 lowercase hex> <YYYY-MM-DD> <reason>
#
# The head is part of the key on purpose. A pinned PR that gets a PUSH has a
# new head and a freshly-dispatched verdict — a different fact about a
# different tree — and it reds as NOVEL rather than inheriting this line's
# cover. The reason must be at least 12 characters: say which pull request,
# why it is standing debt, and what would let the line be deleted.
#
# Example (do not uncomment; it names no real pull request):
#   99999 b93cbb768 2026-08-17 abandoned spike kept open for its review thread; delete when it is closed
#
# ── ENTRIES ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# EMPTY, and measured so on 2026-09-01: #11766 — the pull request this watch
# had been red about for fifteen days — was closed at 21:26Z, and a full local
# run over the live population (75 open, 2 CONFLICTING: #14874 and #14825)
# reported ZERO conflicted pull requests asserting a stale green. Nothing is
# owed, so nothing is pinned.
#
# An empty file is not an inert file. With no entries, EVERY stale verdict is
# NOVEL and the watch behaves exactly as it did before the ratchet existed —
# which is why this change cannot launder anything today. The mechanism is
# here for the next #11766, and the healed arm above is what will stop it from
# being left switched on afterwards.
